4.1  Process states and zombies

TL;DR
  - Every process has a one-letter state: R running, S sleeping, D waiting on
    I/O, T stopped, Z zombie.
  - Most processes are S, waiting for something. That's normal.
  - A zombie has already exited. Only its entry is left, until its parent reads
    how it ended.
  - Zombies use no CPU and almost no memory, but each one holds a PID.
  - You can't kill a zombie: fix or restart its parent.

      ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm | awk '$3 ~ /^Z/'

On your machine
  Total     8 processes: 1 running, 3 sleeping, 1 waiting on I/O (D), 1 idle
            kernel thread, 2 zombies
  Zombies   PID 4242 worker (old), parent 4100 supervisor
            PID 4300 orphan-job, parent 9999
  Waiting   PID 5000 rsync, parent 1 systemd

Pitfalls
  - kill -9 on a zombie does nothing: it's already dead. The PPID column tells
    you which parent to look at.
  - A few zombies that come and go are normal. A count that keeps growing means
    a parent that never collects its children.
  - A process in D usually ignores even kill -9 until its I/O finishes. Many of
    them at once often points to a slow disk or a hung network mount.
  - D processes count in the load average, so the load can be high while the
    CPUs sit idle.
  - ps adds letters after the state: s session leader, + foreground, < high
    priority, N low priority, l multithreaded.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why do zombies exist at all?

    When a process exits, the kernel frees its memory and closes its files, but
    keeps a small record: its PID, its exit code and the resources it used. The
    parent collects that record with the wait() family of system calls. Until it
    does, the dead child shows up as a zombie (<defunct> in ps).

    Without this record, a parent couldn't learn whether its child succeeded,
    failed, or was killed by a signal. Once the parent reads it, the entry
    disappears: the child has been "reaped".

  ▾ What happens to children when their parent dies?

    They become orphans and are adopted by PID 1 (usually systemd), or by the
    closest ancestor that registered itself as a "subreaper" (possible since
    kernel 3.4). PID 1 collects its adopted children, so their zombies vanish.
    That's why stopping the parent of a zombie gets rid of it.

    Containers are the classic trap: if your app runs as PID 1 and never calls
    wait(), zombies pile up. A tiny init such as tini (what docker run --init
    adds) fixes that.

  ▾ What's the difference between S and D?

    Both are sleeping. In S (interruptible sleep), a signal wakes the process
    up: Ctrl-C works. In D (uninterruptible sleep), the kernel holds signals
    back, because stopping halfway through would leave something inconsistent,
    usually a disk read or write inside a driver.

    D normally lasts milliseconds. When it lasts minutes, the cause is below the
    process: an NFS server that stopped answering, a failing disk, or a driver
    bug. Since kernel 2.6.25 some waits are "killable": kill -9 gets through,
    even though ps still shows D.

Related
  Commands  ps, top
  Paths     /proc/PID/stat, /proc/PID/status
  See also  idk 4.2 Signals
  See also  idk 7.1 Load average
